Reject a seconds-since-the-epoch time that localtime() can't convert.
The Microsoft runtime's localtime() returns NULL for a time before 1970
or after the year 3000. parse_datestr() checks for that in the @seconds
form, and strptime() does for %s, but both go through the localtime()
wrapper (strptime() by way of localtime_r()), which substitutes the
epoch for such a time so that it never returns NULL. So date -d @-86400,
date -d @99999999999, touch -d @-1, and date -D %s with too large a
number all silently used 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, printing it, setting
the clock to it, or giving it to the file.

Add localtime_or_null(), the runtime's localtime() without the
substitution, and use it in these two places, so that such a time is
reported as an invalid date like any other time out of range.

